1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
A collaborative study from several countries followed nearly

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,120
10,000 women who carry a harmful version of

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:10,440
a gene called BRCA1 or BRCA2.

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,720
Among the BRCA1 carriers, about 72 in every

5
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,600
100 were estimated to get breast cancer by

6
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:18,760
age 80.

7
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:21,520
It's exactly the kind of large shared project

8
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,080
we met in the last video.

9
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:25,840
So does carrying that variant mean a woman

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,360
will get breast cancer?

11
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,560
And can population genetics predict anything with real

12
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:31,440
accuracy?

13
00:00:32,667 --> 00:00:34,666
Here's what that number really says.

14
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:38,546
Picture 100 carriers as 100 dots.

15
00:00:38,827 --> 00:00:42,426
About 72 would develop breast cancer by 80,

16
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:44,226
and about 28 wouldn't.

17
00:00:44,507 --> 00:00:46,986
The study predicts that proportion well, because it

18
00:00:47,067 --> 00:00:49,066
was measured across thousands of people.

19
00:00:49,707 --> 00:00:52,026
Follow another large group of carriers, and you'd

20
00:00:52,107 --> 00:00:54,626
expect close to the same proportion again.

21
00:00:54,907 --> 00:00:57,626
That's a risk pattern, a trend in how

22
00:00:57,707 --> 00:01:00,026
likely something is across a group.

23
00:01:00,107 --> 00:01:02,266
What it can't say is which dots are

24
00:01:02,467 --> 00:01:03,146
which.

25
00:01:03,227 --> 00:01:05,826
For one woman, it's a probability, not a

26
00:01:05,867 --> 00:01:06,666
forecast.

27
00:01:07,867 --> 00:01:09,866
So why can't the gene decide on its

28
00:01:09,947 --> 00:01:10,346
own?

29
00:01:10,987 --> 00:01:14,506
BRCA1 makes a protein that helps repair damaged

30
00:01:14,587 --> 00:01:15,426
DNA.

31
00:01:15,707 --> 00:01:18,586
A carrier inherits one faulty copy, but her

32
00:01:18,667 --> 00:01:20,386
other copy still works.

33
00:01:20,667 --> 00:01:23,626
Cancer usually starts only if a cell also

34
00:01:23,707 --> 00:01:26,746
loses that working copy, and whether that happens

35
00:01:26,827 --> 00:01:29,866
depends on chance damage, other genes and other

36
00:01:29,947 --> 00:01:32,386
factors, like hormones and age.

37
00:01:32,667 --> 00:01:34,746
So the variant raises the risk.

38
00:01:35,067 --> 00:01:36,826
It doesn't fix the outcome.

39
00:01:38,034 --> 00:01:41,473
So a higher probability isn't a certain outcome.

40
00:01:41,954 --> 00:01:43,633
And it works the other way too.

41
00:01:43,954 --> 00:01:46,433
Most women who get breast cancer don't carry

42
00:01:46,474 --> 00:01:49,553
a BRCA variant at all, because these variants

43
00:01:49,634 --> 00:01:52,033
are rare and breast cancer has many other

44
00:01:52,114 --> 00:01:53,233
causes.

45
00:01:53,314 --> 00:01:56,273
So testing negative for a BRCA variant isn't

46
00:01:56,354 --> 00:01:57,673
a guarantee either.

47
00:01:57,954 --> 00:02:00,753
Genetic risk is not genetic destiny.

48
00:02:01,967 --> 00:02:04,686
Population predictions can be very accurate, though.

49
00:02:05,167 --> 00:02:07,006
Here's one that health services use.

50
00:02:07,647 --> 00:02:11,406
Cystic fibrosis is recessive, so carriers look healthy

51
00:02:11,527 --> 00:02:13,566
and can't be counted by looking.

52
00:02:13,607 --> 00:02:16,206
But affected babies can be counted.

53
00:02:16,367 --> 00:02:20,126
Say one baby in 2,500 is affected.

54
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:22,526
An affected baby needs the allele from both

55
00:02:22,807 --> 00:02:26,366
parents, so the allele's frequency times itself is

56
00:02:26,447 --> 00:02:28,766
one in 2,500.

57
00:02:28,887 --> 00:02:31,246
Take the square root, and the allele's frequency

58
00:02:31,367 --> 00:02:36,766
is 0.02, so the normal allele's is 0.98.

59
00:02:36,847 --> 00:02:38,846
Carriers have one of each, and there are

60
00:02:38,927 --> 00:02:40,926
two ways to get that, from the mother

61
00:02:41,167 --> 00:02:42,206
or the father.

62
00:02:42,527 --> 00:02:47,326
So carriers are two times 0.98 times 0.02,

63
00:02:47,407 --> 00:02:50,046
about one person in 25.

64
00:02:50,127 --> 00:02:53,086
This is the Hardy-Weinberg principle, and it shows

65
00:02:53,167 --> 00:02:55,966
how a rare disease can have common carriers.

66
00:02:57,167 --> 00:02:59,806
But that estimate only holds if the population

67
00:02:59,887 --> 00:03:03,006
is large, mates at random, and isn't changed

68
00:03:03,087 --> 00:03:06,286
by selection, migration or new mutations.

69
00:03:06,607 --> 00:03:09,486
Real populations rarely meet all of these, and

70
00:03:09,567 --> 00:03:10,446
that's useful too.

71
00:03:11,007 --> 00:03:13,486
If the real numbers differ from the prediction,

72
00:03:13,567 --> 00:03:15,726
something is acting on the population.

73
00:03:16,207 --> 00:03:19,006
For example, if DNA tests give the allele's

74
00:03:19,087 --> 00:03:21,646
frequency, and more babies are affected than it

75
00:03:21,887 --> 00:03:24,686
predicts, relatives may be having children together.

76
00:03:25,487 --> 00:03:28,046
In a large population over a short time,

77
00:03:28,327 --> 00:03:29,486
predictions come close.

78
00:03:30,047 --> 00:03:32,686
In a small population, chance can push allele

79
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:36,766
frequencies either way, so long-range predictions there fail.

80
00:03:37,967 --> 00:03:40,846
Genetic risk is also shared within families.

81
00:03:41,407 --> 00:03:44,686
If a woman carries a BRCA1 variant, each

82
00:03:44,767 --> 00:03:46,446
of her children has a one in two

83
00:03:46,527 --> 00:03:48,846
chance of inheriting it, and so does each

84
00:03:48,887 --> 00:03:51,006
of her brothers and sisters, because one of

85
00:03:51,087 --> 00:03:54,126
her parents almost certainly carries it too.

86
00:03:54,207 --> 00:03:56,846
So genetic data are never only about one

87
00:03:56,927 --> 00:03:58,286
person.

88
00:03:58,367 --> 00:04:02,206
In 2003, Iceland's Supreme Court upheld a woman's

89
00:04:02,367 --> 00:04:05,166
claim that her late father's health records shouldn't

90
00:04:05,247 --> 00:04:08,206
go into a planned national database, because data

91
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:11,166
about him could reveal information about her.

92
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:15,120
So in an exam, match your claim to

93
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:16,240
your evidence.

94
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:20,000
Strong answers say the data suggest a trend,

95
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:23,120
indicate increased risk, or make an outcome more

96
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,720
likely in this population.

97
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:28,640
Weak answers say the data prove every person

98
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:31,920
will be affected, guarantee an outcome, or remove

99
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,360
all uncertainty.

100
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:35,840
So don't write that carriers will get the

101
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:36,480
disease.

102
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:39,120
Write that carriers are more likely to get

103
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:39,760
it.

104
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:42,640
Be precise and cautious at the same time.

105
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:45,546
Three lines to remember.

106
00:04:45,867 --> 00:04:49,426
Population evidence gives population conclusions.

107
00:04:49,707 --> 00:04:52,826
Genetic risk is not genetic destiny.

108
00:04:53,067 --> 00:04:56,106
And for a rare recessive condition, expect far

109
00:04:56,187 --> 00:04:58,426
more carriers than affected people.

110
00:04:59,634 --> 00:05:01,353
Two harder questions.

111
00:05:01,634 --> 00:05:05,153
First, a study of 50,000 adults finds that

112
00:05:05,274 --> 00:05:08,113
carriers of a variant develop heart disease twice

113
00:05:08,194 --> 00:05:09,873
as often as non-carriers.

114
00:05:10,594 --> 00:05:13,713
A report about one carrier says: this person

115
00:05:13,954 --> 00:05:15,393
will get heart disease.

116
00:05:16,114 --> 00:05:17,553
What does the study support?

117
00:05:18,194 --> 00:05:20,993
Rewrite the claim so it matches the evidence.

118
00:05:21,314 --> 00:05:22,673
Pause here to answer.

119
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:36,460
The study supports a group trend: carriers, as

120
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:38,780
a group, have about twice the risk.

121
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:41,500
It can't say what happens to one person,

122
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:45,100
because other genes, environment and chance also matter.

123
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:48,540
A better claim: this person has a higher

124
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:50,140
risk than a non-carrier.

125
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:52,380
And if you said twice the risk means

126
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:55,420
a fifty per cent chance, twice a small

127
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:57,180
risk can still be small.

128
00:05:58,400 --> 00:05:58,880
Second.

129
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:03,360
A recessive condition affects one baby in 10,000.

130
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:07,440
Assuming Hardy-Weinberg, estimate the carrier frequency.

131
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,080
Then explain why the estimate would be less

132
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:13,360
reliable in a small, isolated village.

133
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:14,720
Pause here to answer.

134
00:06:25,134 --> 00:06:29,613
The allele's frequency, squared, is one in 10,000,

135
00:06:29,694 --> 00:06:33,453
so the allele's frequency is 0.01, and the

136
00:06:33,534 --> 00:06:36,653
normal allele's is 0.99.

137
00:06:36,734 --> 00:06:41,613
Carriers are two times 0.99 times 0.01,

138
00:06:41,694 --> 00:06:45,733
about 0.02, or one person in 50.

139
00:06:46,174 --> 00:06:48,893
In a small village, chance and marriages between

140
00:06:48,974 --> 00:06:50,973
relatives break the assumptions.

141
00:06:51,214 --> 00:06:53,613
And if you said one in 10,000, that's

142
00:06:53,654 --> 00:06:56,253
the affected babies, not the carriers.

143
00:06:57,467 --> 00:07:01,546
So, population genetics predicts group patterns well: risk

144
00:07:01,627 --> 00:07:04,906
across a group, carrier frequencies, how alleles are

145
00:07:05,107 --> 00:07:07,466
spread, and how related populations are.

146
00:07:07,947 --> 00:07:10,626
It can't fix one person's future.

147
00:07:11,067 --> 00:07:13,786
Module 5 showed how inheritance works and how

148
00:07:13,867 --> 00:07:14,586
to predict it.

149
00:07:15,147 --> 00:07:18,186
Next, in Module 6, we'll see how mutation

150
00:07:18,227 --> 00:07:20,586
and technology change these patterns.

151
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:23,240
Here are the key notes.

152
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:25,400
Pause the video if you need more time

153
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:26,280
to copy them.
